\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces NM-ROM model and online solve. \textbf  {Top, trained once and then frozen:} the latent code $z$ passes through a linear skip and a shallow SiLU network, and their sum gives the bank coefficients $h(z)$. The ordered coordinate-network bank, multiplied by a factor that vanishes on the boundary $\Gamma $, turns coefficients into the field $\tilde  u$ with the Dirichlet data exact. A query uses either the head, $\tilde  u=G h(z)$, or the span of the first $R'$ ordered columns, $\tilde  u=\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle G$}\mathaccent "0362{G}_{R'}c$. \textbf  {Bottom, solved at each query:} the residual of $\tilde  u$ is tested against $M$ fixed tests $P$. Linear terms use the matrix $PKG$, computed once, and are exact; Burgers advection uses the empirical quadrature rule on $N_{\mathrm  {eq}}$ nodes. On a linear PDE in the span the tested residual is linear in $c$ and is solved once by least squares; otherwise Levenberg--Marquardt steps update $z$ or $c$ until the stopping test is met. Dashed tags mark the deployment knobs: the bank width $R'$ sets the accuracy, and the quadrature rule and the stopping tolerance set the cost.}}{6}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:decoder}{{1}{6}{NM-ROM model and online solve. \textbf {Top, trained once and then frozen:} the latent code $z$ passes through a linear skip and a shallow SiLU network, and their sum gives the bank coefficients $\head (z)$. The ordered coordinate-network bank, multiplied by a factor that vanishes on the boundary $\Gamma $, turns coefficients into the field $\tilde u$ with the Dirichlet data exact. A query uses either the head, $\tilde u=G h(z)$, or the span of the first $R'$ ordered columns, $\tilde u=\widehat G_{R'}c$. \textbf {Bottom, solved at each query:} the residual of $\tilde u$ is tested against $M$ fixed tests $P$. Linear terms use the matrix $PKG$, computed once, and are exact; Burgers advection uses the empirical quadrature rule on $N_{\mathrm {eq}}$ nodes. On a linear PDE in the span the tested residual is linear in $c$ and is solved once by least squares; otherwise Levenberg--Marquardt steps update $z$ or $c$ until the stopping test is met. Dashed tags mark the deployment knobs: the bank width $R'$ sets the accuracy, and the quadrature rule and the stopping tolerance set the cost}{figure.caption.16}{}}
