\newlabel{tab:headline-times}{{C.4}{19}{Supporting data for Table~\ref {tab:headline}, including the full-order setting the rule selected for each row: the two settings of each frozen model, median times (ms), timing scope and status. ``EQ'' is the lattice rule of Appendix~\ref {app:method:eq}. Where a run also recorded the complete query (host transfer included), those speedups are listed. Heat errors are measured against the exact time evolution on the same grid, so the NM-ROM and CN--CG errors both include the Crank--Nicolson time error}{table.caption.46}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {C.5}{\ignorespaces Solver iterations per query and where the query time goes. On Poisson and heat the query is a direct solve; \emph  {input} is projecting the forcing or fitting the initial state, \emph  {solve} the reduced solve including all time steps, and \emph  {output} rebuilding the field at the output times. These stage times come from separately compiled stages in the same jobs, so they do not add exactly to the query time. Burgers: Levenberg--Marquardt iterations per query, median over the cases (largest number in one step in brackets): in two dimensions over 50 time steps of the six development cases, every step stopped on the convergence test; in three dimensions over 50 ($\Delta t=0.005$) or 25 ($\Delta t=0.01$) steps of the 32 held-out cases. Navier--Stokes: 10 time steps of three Gauss--Newton sweeps each, plus 12 Gauss--Newton sweeps that fit the head's starting code to the projected initial field, starting from a stored training code. Query times as in Table~\ref {tab:headline-times} (ms).}}{19}{table.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:iterations}{{C.5}{19}{Solver iterations per query and where the query time goes. On Poisson and heat the query is a direct solve; \emph {input} is projecting the forcing or fitting the initial state, \emph {solve} the reduced solve including all time steps, and \emph {output} rebuilding the field at the output times. These stage times come from separately compiled stages in the same jobs, so they do not add exactly to the query time. Burgers: Levenberg--Marquardt iterations per query, median over the cases (largest number in one step in brackets): in two dimensions over 50 time steps of the six development cases, every step stopped on the convergence test; in three dimensions over 50 ($\Delta t=0.005$) or 25 ($\Delta t=0.01$) steps of the 32 held-out cases. Navier--Stokes: 10 time steps of three Gauss--Newton sweeps each, plus 12 Gauss--Newton sweeps that fit the head's starting code to the projected initial field, starting from a stored training code. Query times as in Table~\ref {tab:headline-times} (ms)}{table.caption.47}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {C.6}{\ignorespaces Supporting data for Table~\ref {tab:nmrom-baselines}: every row of that table with its residual path and median GPU query time from the same allocation. Our rows use the unoptimised dense reference path (no empirical quadrature, stopping tolerance $10^{-6}$); our optimised query is timed only in Table~\ref {tab:headline}, in other jobs, and no ratio across jobs is formed. The shallow masked-autoencoder NM-LSPG of \citet  {KimChoi2022NMROM} passed its reproduction gate, on their own 2D Burgers benchmark (their Sec.~6.2), on the third of three pre-registered attempts (sigmoid{} activation; median $1.45\,\%$ against a $1.5\,\%$ bar; published ${<}1\,\%$). Its published encoder width ($2n$) needs $136$\,GB to train at $256^2$ and $2183$\,GB at $512^2$, beyond the $77$ and $135$\,GB devices; at $512^2$ its encoder, capped at width $4096$, trained only $229\mbox  {--}231$ epochs in its $4800$\,s budget, so those errors partly reflect a training-time limit. The data-matched row is trained on our bank's 576{} trajectories.}}{20}{table.caption.48}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:nmrom-baselines-appx}{{C.6}{20}{Supporting data for Table~\ref {tab:nmrom-baselines}: every row of that table with its residual path and median GPU query time from the same allocation. Our rows use the unoptimised dense reference path (no empirical quadrature, stopping tolerance $10^{-6}$); our optimised query is timed only in Table~\ref {tab:headline}, in other jobs, and no ratio across jobs is formed. The shallow masked-autoencoder NM-LSPG of \citet {KimChoi2022NMROM} passed its reproduction gate, on their own 2D Burgers benchmark (their Sec.~6.2), on the third of three pre-registered attempts (\nBaseGateAct {} activation; median $\nBaseGateMedian \,\%$ against a $\nBaseGateBar \,\%$ bar; published ${<}\nBaseGatePublished \,\%$). Its published encoder width ($2n$) needs $\nBaseEncoderNeedGB $\,GB to train at $256^2$ and $\nBaseEncoderNeedGBFiveTwelve $\,GB at $512^2$, beyond the $\nBaseDeviceGB $ and $\nBaseDeviceGBFiveTwelve $\,GB devices; at $512^2$ its encoder, capped at width $\nBaseKimWidth $, trained only $\nBaseKimEpochs $ epochs in its $\nBaseKimWall $\,s budget, so those errors partly reflect a training-time limit. The data-matched row is trained on our bank's \nBaseDataMatched {} trajectories}{table.caption.48}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {C.7}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional configurations, read from the run records. Domains are the unit cube with homogeneous Dirichlet data, except Navier--Stokes, which is periodic.}}{20}{table.caption.49}\protected@file@percent }
