\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D.1}{\ignorespaces Bank-width tunability at the finest mesh. Each block is one frozen model, and within a cohort every speedup divides the median time of \emph  {one} full-order setting (the \emph  {FOM} row, the fastest at least as accurate as the block's most accurate setting, in the same allocation) by the setting's time (ms). Error is the worst same-grid relative $L^2$ error (\%), over evolved times for Navier--Stokes and Burgers and over all times for heat. Held-out cases were never used for selection and share the development settings; for Burgers 3D the development column is the 64 validation cases on which its settings were chosen, and only the two selected widths were run on the held-out cases. The Burgers 2D held-out cases ran on an A100 GPU and the development cases on an H200 GPU. Heat is shown at $2048^2$, the finest mesh whose timing is not provisional.}}{21}{table.caption.51}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:tunability}{{D.1}{21}{Bank-width tunability at the finest mesh. Each block is one frozen model, and within a cohort every speedup divides the median time of \emph {one} full-order setting (the \emph {FOM} row, the fastest at least as accurate as the block's most accurate setting, in the same allocation) by the setting's time (ms). Error is the worst same-grid relative $L^2$ error (\%), over evolved times for Navier--Stokes and Burgers and over all times for heat. Held-out cases were never used for selection and share the development settings; for Burgers 3D the development column is the 64 validation cases on which its settings were chosen, and only the two selected widths were run on the held-out cases. The Burgers 2D held-out cases ran on an A100 GPU and the development cases on an H200 GPU. Heat is shown at $2048^2$, the finest mesh whose timing is not provisional}{table.caption.51}{}}
